1. Containers and Kubernetes


What is a Container
A container is a lightweight package that includes:
•	Application code
•	Runtime
•	Libraries
•	Dependencies
•	Configuration files
This package can run anywhere where a container runtime exists.
Simple Definition
A container = application + dependencies packaged together so it runs consistently on any system.
Example
Suppose you build a Java web application.
Normally you need:
•	Java installed
•	Libraries installed
•	Correct OS configuration
With containers:
•	Everything is inside the container image
•	You just run the container.
________________________________________

Container Image
A container image is the blueprint used to create containers.
Example images:
•	nginx
•	mysql
•	ubuntu
Command example (container runtime):
podman run nginx
or
docker run nginx
This command:
•	pulls nginx image
•	creates a container
•	runs nginx server
________________________________________
Linux Technologies Used by Containers
1. Namespaces
Namespaces isolate processes.
Each container has:
•	its own filesystem
•	its own network
•	its own processes
So containers cannot see each other.
________________________________________
2. Control Groups (cgroups)
Used for resource management.
Controls:
•	CPU usage
•	Memory usage
•	Disk IO
Example:
Container can be limited to 500MB RAM.
________________________________________
Open Container Initiative (OCI)
OCI defines standards for containers.
It standardizes:
•	container image format
•	container runtime
Because of OCI:
•	container images run anywhere.
Example runtimes:
•	Podman
•	Docker
•	CRI-O
•	containerd
________________________________________
2. Kubernetes Overview
What is Kubernetes
Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform.
It automates:
•	container deployment
•	scaling
•	networking
•	monitoring
•	management
Simple definition
Kubernetes manages containers across many servers automatically.
________________________________________
Why Kubernetes is Needed
If you run containers manually:
Problems:
•	container crashes
•	server failure
•	scaling manually
•	networking complexity
Kubernetes solves these problems.
________________________________________
Kubernetes Cluster
A cluster is a group of machines.
Two types of nodes exist:
1 Control Plane Nodes
These manage the cluster.
Responsibilities:
•	scheduling
•	cluster state
•	API communication
________________________________________
2 Compute Nodes (Worker Nodes)
These run applications.
They execute:
•	pods
•	containers
________________________________________
Pod
A Pod is the smallest unit in Kubernetes.
Definition:
A pod is one or more containers running together on the same node.
Containers inside a pod:
•	share network
•	share storage
Example:
Pod:
Pod
 ├── container 1 (web server)
 └── container 2 (logging agent)
________________________________________
3. Kubernetes Features
1 Service Discovery and Load Balancing
Pods have dynamic IP addresses.
So Kubernetes provides Services.
Service:
•	stable IP
•	stable DNS name
•	load balancing
Example:
frontend-service → multiple frontend pods
Traffic automatically distributed.
Command example:
kubectl get svc
or in OpenShift
oc get svc
________________________________________
2 Horizontal Scaling
Kubernetes can automatically:
•	increase pods
•	decrease pods
Example:
Traffic increases → create more pods.
Traffic decreases → remove pods.
Command example:
kubectl scale deployment nginx --replicas=5
This creates 5 pods.
________________________________________
3 Self Healing
Kubernetes automatically:
•	restarts crashed containers
•	replaces failed pods
•	reschedules pods if node fails
Example:
Pod crashes →
Kubernetes automatically creates new pod.
________________________________________
4 Automated Rollout (Updates)
Kubernetes supports rolling updates.
Example:
Update application version:
Old pods replaced gradually with new pods.
If update fails → rollback.
Command example:
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx
Rollback:
kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx
________________________________________
5 Secrets and Configuration Management
Applications need configuration such as:
•	database password
•	API keys
•	environment variables
Kubernetes provides:
ConfigMap
For normal configuration.
Secret
For sensitive data.
Example command:
Create secret:
kubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=password=1234
Important:
Kubernetes does NOT encrypt secrets by default.
________________________________________
6 Operators
Operators are automated applications that manage complex systems.
Example:
Database deployment requires:
•	replication
•	backup
•	scaling
•	failover
Operator automates everything.
Example:
PostgreSQL Operator automatically:
•	installs database
•	configures replication
•	manages backup
________________________________________
4. Kubernetes Architecture
Cluster contains nodes (servers).
Nodes can be:
•	physical servers
•	virtual machines
•	cloud instances
________________________________________
Control Plane Components
Control plane manages cluster.
Important components:
API Server
Main interface of Kubernetes.
All commands go through it.
Example:
kubectl create pod
This request goes to API server.
________________________________________
Scheduler
Decides which node runs a pod.
Example:
Pod requires:
•	2 CPU
•	2GB RAM
Scheduler chooses node with available resources.
________________________________________
Controller Manager
Controllers ensure cluster state.
Example:
If deployment requires 3 pods
Controller ensures 3 pods always run.
________________________________________
Compute Node Components
Worker nodes run applications.
Components:
Kubelet
Agent running on node.
Responsibilities:
•	communicate with API server
•	run containers
________________________________________
Container Runtime
Software that runs containers.
Examples:
•	CRI-O
•	containerd
•	Podman
________________________________________
5. Kubernetes API and Resource Model
Kubernetes works with resources.
Examples of resources:
•	Pod
•	Service
•	Deployment
•	ConfigMap
•	Secret
All resources use same API.
________________________________________
kubectl / oc Commands
Two CLI tools exist.
Kubernetes
kubectl
Example:
kubectl get pods
________________________________________
OpenShift
oc
Example:
oc get pods
OpenShift uses Kubernetes internally but provides extra tools.
________________________________________
6. Declarative Resource Management
Kubernetes follows declarative configuration.
Instead of giving step-by-step commands, you define desired state.
Example YAML file:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
Create pod:
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
Kubernetes ensures pod exists.
________________________________________
Controllers Maintain Desired State
Controllers continuously check cluster state.
Example:
Desired state:
replicas: 3
If one pod crashes →
Controller creates new pod automatically.
________________________________________
7. Namespaces
Namespaces are used to organize resources in a cluster.
Example:
Different teams can use different namespaces.
Example namespaces:
•	dev
•	testing
•	production
Command:
List namespaces:
kubectl get namespaces
Create namespace:
kubectl create namespace dev
Create pod inside namespace:
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx -n dev
